Reason:
<text>

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
md->dax_dev = alloc_dax(md, &dm_dax_ops);
if (IS_ERR(md->dax_dev)) {
    md->dax_dev = NULL;
    goto bad;
}
```
Reason:
The `alloc_dax()` function's return value is treated as a fatal error if it returns an error code (specifically, if it is an error pointer). This includes the `-EOPNOTSUPP` error, which indicates that the operation is not supported. As a result, the allocation process is terminated prematurely, even when it could continue without DAX support.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
dax_dev = alloc_dax(md, &dm_dax_ops);
if (IS_ERR(dax_dev)) {
    if (PTR_ERR(dax_dev) != -EOPNOTSUPP)
        goto bad;
} else {
    set_dax_nocache(dax_dev);
    set_dax_nomc(dax_dev);
    md->dax_dev = dax_dev;
```
Reason:
The patch modifies the error handling to check if the error returned by `alloc_dax()` is specifically `-EOPNOTSUPP`. If it is, the code does not treat it as a fatal error and allows the allocation process to continue. This change ensures that the device allocation can proceed even when DAX support is not available, thus preventing unnecessary failures.

